**Code Explanation:**

The code performs an analysis of single-particle dispersion in the Kraichnan model across different spectral roughness parameters (xi). It loads the trajectory datasets and the corresponding time grid, aligning them to compute the Mean Squared Displacement (MSD) over time. To avoid logarithmic singularities, the initial time step (t=0) is excluded. For each dataset, the local scaling exponent H(t) is computed using the gradient of the log-MSD with respect to log-time. The global scaling exponent H is then extracted by performing a linear regression in the asymptotic regime (defined here as t > 50) in log-log space. The residuals of this fit are calculated to verify the linearity and convergence in the chosen asymptotic range. The empirical fractional Laplacian exponent alpha is derived from the global scaling exponent using the relation alpha = 1/H, and compared against the theoretical prediction alpha = 2/xi. Two figures are generated: 1. A three-panel plot showing the MSD vs. time (log-log scale), the local scaling exponent H(t) vs. time (semilog-x scale), and the fit residuals in the asymptotic regime (semilog-x scale). 2. A dedicated comparison plot of the empirical alpha values against the theoretical predictions, including a 1:1 reference line to assess the agreement. All quantitative results, including the theoretical and empirical values for H and alpha, are printed to the console for further interpretation.

**Modifications:**

The code was structured to follow the required format, ensuring all top-level execution is within the if __name__ == '__main__': block. File paths were corrected to use the data/ directory for output, and the script was verified to avoid command-line arguments and LaTeX rendering.

